# CESC 410L — labs_and_projects
# Scoped to this folder. The repo-root .gitignore stays untouched.

# --- Virtual environments -------------------------------------------------
# uv creates .venv by default. ~254 MB with matplotlib/numpy/scipy installed,
# so this is the single most important line here.
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/
.uv/

# If a lab or the instructor has us create a venv under a different name,
# ADD IT HERE and note why in that lab's README. Do not rely on the patterns
# above catching it — they only match these exact names.
# e.g.  dsp-env/
#       .venv312/

# --- Python bytecode and build artifacts ----------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/

# --- Test / tooling caches ------------------------------------------------
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# --- Jupyter --------------------------------------------------------------
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# --- Editors / OS ---------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- Generated figures ----------------------------------------------------
# Ignored: they are regenerated by one command and would otherwise bloat the
# repo (~480 KB per lab per run, and a new set every time the ERAU ID or a
# parameter changes).
#
# Safe to ignore because regeneration is deterministic: myID is committed in
# the lab source and uv.lock pins matplotlib, so the same command reproduces
# the same figures.
#
#     cd dsp26 && MPLBACKEND=Agg uv run dsp26 <command> --folder-name ../labNN
#
# To keep a specific set anyway -- the exact figures submitted, say:
#     git add -f labNN/figs/

# The SAME reasoning applies to a second figure folder when a lab needs one --
# Lab 1 has figs_music/ for the experimental task's own recording. Regenerable
# from the committed clip by the command in that lab's README.
figs_*/

# ...but NOT figs_player/. That is a screenshot of a running GUI window, taken
# by hand. Nothing regenerates it: no headless path can produce it, and losing
# it means opening the application and capturing it again. The handout requires
# it as an artifact, so it is tracked deliberately.
!figs_player/
!figs_player/**
figs/

# --- LaTeX build artifacts ------------------------------------------------
*.aux
*.log
*.out
*.toc
*.synctex.gz
*.fls
*.fdb_latexmk

# Rendered reports. tools/render_reports.sh KEEPS these now -- they are
# ignored here instead of deleted, so the built report is on hand without a
# rebuild and still never enters git.
#
# Deliberately narrow: it matches report.pdf / report-v2.pdf and NOT an
# instructor handout like dsp-ba--lab0-getting-started-26.pdf. Do not widen
# this to *.pdf, which would silently drop handouts from the repo.
report*.pdf
# artifacts.pdf is the same build product under another name. The SUBMITTED
# copy (labNN-artifacts-lastname-firstname.pdf) is committed instead, so the
# repo carries the document once, not twice.
artifacts.pdf

# --- Overleaf copies ------------------------------------------------------
# Self-contained copies of a report, generated by flattening the .tex. They are
# regenerable and the .tex source is tracked, so they never enter git.
#
# These are for pasting into Overleaf ONLY. Do not feed one to the packaging
# script: the flattener stamps the source path into the file's header, and the
# submission guard rejects a repository path in anything about to ship.
overleaf/

# --- Archives -------------------------------------------------------------
# Every zip is a build product of tools/make_submission.sh -- rebuildable,
# and often megabytes. Nothing zipped belongs in git.
*.zip

# --- Deliberately NOT ignored ---------------------------------------------
# uv.lock          -> commit it; it is what makes the environment reproducible
# pyproject.toml   -> commit it
# labNN/*.tex      -> the report source IS the artifact
# labNN/README.md  -> the reproduction log
# instructor PDFs  -> handouts are inputs, not build output (the report*.pdf
#                     rule above is deliberately narrow so it cannot match
#                     a handout like dsp-ba--lab0-getting-started-26.pdf)

# Source audio a student supplies for an experimental task. Kept on disk and
# NEVER modified -- the lab cuts a derived clip from it -- but not committed:
# it is multi-megabyte commercial audio, and the repo publishes to a public
# site. The derived clip (music_clip.wav, ~320 KB, mono 16 kHz) IS committed,
# because it is the actual lab input and regenerating it needs the source.
*.mp3
*.m4a
*.flac
